<description>
Swing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.

Input structure:
- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).

Rule:
- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.
- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.
- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.

Key insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="60d73be6" lines="22-39">
<description>
Complete the sparse colored marker pattern by reflecting it across a full-row/full-column cross.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background and one non-background cross color that
fills exactly one complete row and one complete column. All other foreground
pixels are sparse markers away from that cross.

Rule
Keep the input unchanged, and for each marker pixel copy its color to the
positions obtained by reflecting it across the cross row, across the cross
column, and across both axes. Reflected positions outside the grid are
clipped.

Key insight
The full cross is not a copied object; it is the pair of mirror axes for
completing the quadrant pattern.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="83eb0a57" lines="72-86">
<description>
Crop the largest object and overlay each smaller sprite where its shared marker pattern matches.

Input structure
- The background is the most common color, and the foreground has one largest destination object plus smaller source sprites.
- Each source sprite shares exactly one marker color with the destination object.

Rule
- Output the tight bbox crop of the largest object.
- Translate each source sprite so every connected marker component of the shared color exactly matches destination marker components.
- Paste source sprites in decreasing area order, so smaller sprites can sit on top of larger ones when they overlap.

Key insight
- The shared-color marker components are alignment keys; matching whole marker components distinguishes singleton slots from larger same-color marker blocks.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="9f669b64" lines="26-48">
<description>
Push the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one
  horizontal or vertical axis.
- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.
- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular
  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.

Rule
- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is
  flush with the grid edge in that direction.
- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.
- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and
  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.
- Keep the other end object unchanged.

Key insight
- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels
  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="320afe60" lines="41-56">
<description>
Slide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.

Input structure:
- Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color.
- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.
- Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.

Rule:
- If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green.
- Otherwise slide the object to the left edge and recolor it red.
- Start from a blank background canvas; no moved objects overlap.

Key insight:
- The target side is encoded by the object's shape, not by its original position: open notches go right, while closed or solid shapes go left.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="981571dc" lines="6-19">
<description>
Reconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.

Input structure:
- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.
- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.

Rule:
- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.
- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.
- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.

Key insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="880c1354" lines="14-28">
<description>
Recolor the separated outer regions by rotating their colors one step clockwise around the yellow-orange divider.

Input structure:
- The grid contains a fixed yellow/orange divider structure.
- All other colors form separated 4-connected monochrome regions around that divider.

Rule:
- Leave the yellow and orange pixels unchanged.
- Sort the non-divider regions by their centroid angle around the divider centroid in screen-coordinate clockwise order.
- Each region keeps its cells and receives the color of the previous region in that cyclic order.

Key insight:
- The objects do not move; only the color labels cycle through the spatial order of the regions around the divider.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="5545f144" lines="191-207">
<description>
Move a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.

Input structure:
- Frames concatenated horizontally with solid divider columns; bg is most common color.
- Each frame: a triangular arrow-shaped sprite and scattered single-pixel markers, all one color.

Rule:
- Start from the rightmost frame.
- At each step, exactly one cardinal direction from the head has reachable
  markers (on a half-line: same row or column); the sprite advances to the
  nearest one.
- On reaching a marker, consume it; sprite re-faces that direction.
- Repeat until no markers remain; output the final sprite position on a clean bg.

Key insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="78e78cff" lines="71-91">
<description>
Recolor the row-wise passage indicated by wall pixels with the singleton marker color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background contains one singleton marker pixel and one wall color.
- Wall pixels lie on several rows and form left/right limits around the marker column.
- Empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows are open horizontal passages.

Rule:
- On a wall row, paint background pixels between the nearest wall strictly
  left of the marker column and the nearest wall at or right of it; if only
  one side is present, paint from that wall to the opposite grid edge.
- On empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows, paint the whole row.
- On empty rows outside the wall-row band, reuse the nearest end wall-row interval.
- Preserve wall pixels and the original marker pixel.

Key insight:
- The wall color defines scanline limits relative to the marker column; the
  marker color fills those implied horizontal spans rather than flood-filling
  through connected background.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="34cfa167" lines="94-117">
<description>
Complete a four-corner framed rectangle from one decorated anchor rectangle and its diagonal mate.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Two same-color solid rectangular anchors of the same size sit on opposite
  diagonal corners, with the first anchor above-left of the second.
- The upper-left anchor has two one-cell-thick vertical control bars to its
  right and two one-cell-thick horizontal control bars below it.

Rule:
- Copy the anchor rectangle into the two missing corners of the implied
  larger rectangle.
- Repeat the right-side control bars horizontally between the left and right
  anchors, using their spacing as the period, and draw the outer top and
  bottom borders in the first right-control color.
- Repeat the below-side control bars vertically between the top and bottom
  anchors, using the same period, and draw the outer left and right borders
  in the first below-control color.

Key insight: The small control bars are the first two ticks of two periodic
frame patterns; the diagonal anchor supplies the opposite corner and the
missing two anchor copies.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="db0c5428" lines="32-51">
<description>
Expand a 3x3 block motif into a 5x5 block motif around its missing center.

Input structure:
- Teal background surrounds one 9x9 foreground motif.
- The motif is a 3x3 array of 3x3 blocks; the middle block is empty teal.
- Colored pixels immediately around the empty middle block encode the border
  of the missing center block.

Rule:
- Keep the original 9x9 motif in place.
- Copy each of the eight surrounding 3x3 blocks to the opposite outer slot
  of the implied 5x5 block grid, without rotating it.
- Fill the center block from the eight pixels around the hole; its center is
  the motif's majority foreground color.

Key insight: The visible 9x9 motif is the middle of a larger periodic
block-level pattern, and the empty center is reconstructed from its immediate
inner boundary.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="2601afb7" lines="30-45">
<description>
Rotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.

Input structure
- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.
- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.
- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.

Rule
- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.
- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.
- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.

Key insight
- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:
  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

